Really, this entire argument is one of definitions.  I think everyone
agrees that there are people who claim there are gods.  There are
people who claim there are no gods.  And there are people who are
uncertain.  The entire argument is about what to name these people.  I
might suggest this is not a very important argument.

In my experience, people most often use the word "disbelieve" to
express a negative belief.  I disbelieve in gods means I believe there
are no gods.  There are some people who use it in a weaker sense,
indicating merely a lack of positive belief (and saying nothing about
the negative belief).  I think this is the wrong usage, but I'm not
sure, and frankly it doesn't matter much.  Similarly, I think an
Atheist is someone who claims there are no gods.  Practically
everytime I hear the word used, this is the meaning.  But if the word
is used otherwise, it really doesn't matter so long as everyone
understands what is meant.

> > *Note "Disbelief" should not be confused with "Unbelief". They are two 
> > very different things, and can not be used interchangeably.

I agree with this.  I think you are using "disbelief" when you should
be using "unbelief".  But that really doesn't matter.

We could certainly draw a finer line if we wanted so to do.  In the
spectrum of belief, I could make at least the following unique
statements:

1) There are (one or more) gods, and there is no possibility that I am
wrong.
2) There are (one or more) gods, but I admit the possibility that I am
wrong.
3) I make no claim regarding the existance of gods.
4) There are no gods, but I admit the possibility that I am wrong.
5) There are no gods, and there is no possibility that I am wrong.

2 and 4 then are opinions or non-100% certain beliefs.

Some people might say that 2, 3, and 4 are agnostic.  Others might say
that only 3 is agnostic.  Some might say that 4 and 5 are atheistic. 
Others might say only 5 is.  But none of that really matters as long
as we understand to which category a person belongs.  The original
poster that claims to be an "agnostic atheist", I would assume to be
in category 4, although I admit I am not sure.

But my point above all else is that I think almost everybody posting
on this topic agrees on everything of substance, and if we could just
agree on a set of definitions for the sake of argument, we might find
out for sure.

